WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.267
Speculative decoding.

2
00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:07.533
A rare free lunch in serving: faster text with exactly the same output quality.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:12.200
Language models generate one token per forward pass.

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.900
And each pass is memory bandwidth bound.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.757
The GPU spends its time streaming weights, not computing.

6
00:00:18.757 --> 00:00:20.567
That leaves slack to exploit.

7
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:22.833
Here is the trick.

8
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:26.900
A small, fast draft model proposes the next k tokens.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.231
Then the big target model checks all k in a single forward pass.

10
00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:35.767
The bandwidth is already paid, so this costs barely more than one token.

11
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:39.633
We keep the longest prefix the target agrees with, and repeat.

12
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.000
Let's see one round.

13
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.222
The draft proposes four tokens.

14
00:00:44.222 --> 00:00:48.067
The target agrees with the first three, so all three are kept.

15
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:49.988
At the fourth, they disagree.

16
00:00:49.988 --> 00:00:53.900
We take the target's own token, and the draft starts again from there.

17
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.633
Two facts to hold onto.

18
00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:03.628
A clever acceptance rule makes the output distribution provably identical to the target model alone.

19
00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:04.833
Zero quality loss.

20
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.989
And the speedup rides on the acceptance rate.

21
00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:11.733
Formulaic text accepts seventy to eighty percent of drafts.

22
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:15.433
In vLLM it is one extra option.

23
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:19.600
The serve command stays the same, with a speculative config added.

24
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.167
We name a one billion parameter draft model and five tokens per round.

25
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:30.833
The logs then report an acceptance rate of zero point seven four, around two point four times faster.

26
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:33.367
Here is a real case.

27
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:38.833
Mission log summaries are formulaic, exactly what a small draft model predicts well.

28
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:44.233
Add a one billion draft: time per token drops from twenty four to ten milliseconds.

29
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:48.168
And the regression suite shows every eval score identical.

30
00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:49.233
Verify it anyway.

31
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:51.220
Let's recap.

32
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.367
A draft model proposes, and the target verifies.

33
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:57.233
Keep the agreed prefix, and fix the first miss.

34
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.567
The output distribution stays exactly the same.

35
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:03.687
And the speedup follows the acceptance rate.

36
00:02:03.687 --> 00:02:05.033
Next up: CUDA basics.
